**CSS** (Cascading Style Sheets) controls how HTML looks. You can put CSS in a `<style>` tag.

A CSS **rule** has a **selector** (which elements to style) and **declarations** (what to change):

~~~
<style>
  p {
    color: navy;
    font-size: 18px;
  }
</style>
~~~

Each declaration is `property: value;`. Don't forget the semicolon! Colors can be names (`tomato`, `navy`), hex codes (`#ff6347`) and more.

By default, `<div>`s stack on top of each other. **Flexbox** lines children up in a row (or column):

~~~
.row {
  display: flex;
  gap: 12px;
}
~~~

Useful flex properties on the container:

- `gap`: space between children
- `justify-content`: spread along the row (`center`, `space-between`…)
- `align-items`: line up vertically (`center`, `flex-start`…)

Add `flex: 1` to a child to make it stretch and fill the leftover space.

